<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-psoriasis">
  <title>Psoriasis and the Quality of Life</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"The most important practical lesson that can be given to nurses is to teach them what to observe."</p>
      <p>--- Florence Nightingale</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Psoriasis is an unpleasant skin condition with no known cure. The data discussed here come from a phase 3
      clinical trial of a possible treatment.
    </p>
    <p><em>Questions.</em> Is the treatment better than placebo? Does it show better results on all criteria?</p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Wonderful Wednesdays data release by the Statisticians in the Pharmaceutical Industry group.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> Each of 450 patients has two records: quality-of-life assessment at the beginning of the
      study and again at the end.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-psoriasis-groups">
    <title>Comparing treatment and placebo groups</title>

    <p>
      The trial included 300 patients in treatment and 150 in placebo. PASI (Psoriasis Area and Severity Index)
      was assessed at baseline. The DLQI questionnaire (Dermatology Life Quality Index) was administered at baseline
      and again after 16 weeks for almost all participants.
    </p>
    <p>
      The questionnaire has ten items, each scored 0 to 3, with 0 indicating no impact on quality of life and 3
      indicating maximum impact.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-qlens">
      <image source="images/qlEns-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          The spineplot (top left) shows treatment was much larger than placebo and dropout was small in both
          groups. The baseline PASI-DLQI scatterplots (top right) show similar initial distributions. The lower
          panel shows average item scores: both groups start similarly, placebo changes little at week 16, and the
          treatment group improves substantially across all questions.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Inclusion criteria required a PASI baseline at or above a threshold; in this dataset the minimum is 12.
      Only one participant had a baseline score above 50 (the scale maximum is 72).
    </p>
    <p>
      Several DLQI items had higher average baseline burden than others, especially itch/pain, embarrassment,
      and influence on clothing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-psoriasis-questions">
    <title>Examining responses to individual questions</title>

    <introduction>

    <p>
      Treatment improved averages on all questions, but these are ordered categorical responses and averaging is
      only approximate, so individual question distributions are important.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-qlq1">
      <image source="images/qlQ1-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Distributions of answers to question 1 at the start of the trial (top) and at the end (bottom): quality
          of life declines to the right.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      For question 1, placebo distributions are nearly unchanged, while the treatment distribution shifts left,
      indicating broad improvement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Group distributions do not show participant-level transitions directly.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-qlq1t">
      <image source="images/qlQ1t-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Answers to question 1 at the start of the trial (rows) and at the end (columns within the groups).</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Participant-level transitions show that among those starting at severe response levels, most treatment
      participants move to lower scores by week 16, while placebo participants are concentrated in little-change
      categories.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Answers.</em> The treatment group reported better quality of life on all criteria on average. Deeper
      analysis of question 1 showed that most individuals improved.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Further questions.</em> Do other questionnaire items show patterns similar to question 1?
    </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-psoriasis-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Ensembles of graphics can summarise the main features of a study succinctly.</p></li>
        <li><p>Plotting individual scores is more informative than plotting totals alone.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
